  "step_2": "The individual states that the probabilities between Lottery A and B were very similar, so they decided based on the higher payoff. The individual focused on the fact that the probabilities of winning are close (33% vs 34%) and used this similarity to justify prioritizing the higher potential outcome (2500 vs 2400). The justification centers on maximizing the reward given negligible differences in risk. The individual did not mention any reference point, aspiration level, or comparison of outcomes to a calculated average. The deliberation process emphasizes outcome maximization under nearly equal probabilities, not evaluation of outcomes relative to a central aspiration value.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason requires calculating an aspiration level as the mean of all outcomes and then comparing the probabilities of exceeding this level. The relevant information includes the mean outcome (1633.33) and the direction of deviations from it. However, the individual did not reference any such calculation or reference point. Instead, they compared the probabilities and, finding them similar, chose based on the higher maximum outcome. This reflects a 'satisficing' or 'priority heuristic' logic (favoring maximum gain under similar probabilities), but not the aspiration-level rule defined here. There is no evidence that the individual computed an average outcome or evaluated whether outcomes were above or below it. The justification is based purely on outcome size given similar chances, not on positive differences from a central aspiration. Therefore, the decision reason was not used.",
  "final_assessment_score": 10
