{
  "step_1": "The decision reason is based on an aspiration level calculated as the mean of all outcomes across both lotteries, ignoring probabilities. The outcomes in the decision problem are 6000, 0 (from Lottery A), and 3000, 0 (from Lottery B). The unique outcomes are 6000, 3000, and 0. The mean of these outcomes is (6000 + 3000 + 0) / 3 = 3000. This value serves as the aspiration level. The reason evaluates how each outcome in each lottery compares to this aspiration level (3000): outcomes above 3000 yield a positive difference, those equal are neutral, and those below are negative. The reason prefers the lottery with the higher probability of outcomes that exceed the aspiration level (i.e., positive differences). In Lottery A: 6000 > 3000 (positive difference, 45%), 0 < 3000 (negative, 55%). So, probability of positive difference = 45%. In Lottery B: 3000 = 3000 (no positive difference), 0 < 3000 (negative). So, probability of positive difference = 0%. Therefore, the reason would prefer Lottery A (45% > 0%). The decision reason is applicable to this decision problem.",
